# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# aitheros-fleet — the AitherOS fleet HOST role (awnix base, AitherOS role)
#
# Build: podman build -t aitheros-fleet:latest -f Containerfile.aitheros-fleet .
#        (requires awnix-base:latest)
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. Every other image in this family is a WORKSTATION: awnix-base
# hands a stranger a machine an agent can work in, awnix-full adds the whole aw*
# toolkit, awnix-runner adds a CI runner. None of them HOSTS anything — the base
# says so in its own words: "No services. No agent. No account."
#
# The Debian→awnix cutover needs the opposite: a host that runs ~201 quadlets, and
# no image in the family did. That gap is why the cutover had no target, and this
# file is that target.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CONTAIN: the units. Not one. They are GENERATED
# from services.yaml by generate-deploy-units.py, and baking a snapshot of them
# into an image would recreate — permanently, and inside an artifact nobody can
# hand-fix — the exact failure the cutover exists to end. Measured 2026-09-05 on
# the Debian host: 1,017 files in /etc/containers/systemd, of which 201 are units
# and 816 are hand-edit backups from twelve separate in-place edit campaigns. The
# units arrive at deploy time, from source, or they do not arrive.
#
# 🚨 THE NAME IS LOAD-BEARING. This file was first written as
# `Containerfile.awnix-fleet`, and that name is a DISCLOSURE. The boundary in
# AitherOS/config/awnix_boundary.yaml is `aw*` public / `Aither*` commercial, and
# check_awnix_public_lane.py enforces it by EXACT BASENAME against FORBIDDEN. An
# `awnix-` prefix therefore reads as publishable to both the convention and the
# gate — while this file's body names the fleet's internals: 201 quadlets,
# services.yaml, generate-deploy-units, and a retired service that a stray backup
# once resurrected. Renamed to `aitheros-` AND added to FORBIDDEN, because a name
# that merely reads correctly is not enforcement.
#
# Licence: Apache-2.0, same as the base.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
FROM localhost/awnix-base:latest

# ── THE ONE DEPARTURE FROM THE BASE'S STANCE, STATED RATHER THAN SMUGGLED ──────
#
# awnix-base is ROOTLESS-ONLY on purpose and carries no --privileged anywhere. The
# AitherOS fleet as it exists today is the opposite: rootful podman, 201 quadlets
# under /etc/containers/systemd, driven as `podman --root`. Those two facts cannot
# both hold in one image, and the disagreement is an OWNER DECISION, not something
# a Containerfile should quietly settle:
#
#   (a) fleet-host runs ROOTFUL — matches the fleet as it runs today, so the
#       cutover is a move rather than a move plus a re-architecture. This file
#       takes (a) because it is the REVERSIBLE option: a rootful host can adopt
#       rootless later, whereas a cutover that also changes the privilege model
#       cannot be rolled back one half at a time.
#
#   (b) the fleet moves to ROOTLESS as part of the cutover — aligns with awnix's
#       whole security posture, and is a materially larger project: every quadlet,
#       every bind mount under /mnt/c, the podman socket the fleet's own tooling
#       talks to, and the 39 containers that mount config/ would each need
#       revisiting.
#
# Choosing (a) here does NOT make (b) harder later, and the choice is recorded in
# the image itself (see LABEL below) so a future reader does not have to guess
# whether it was decided or defaulted.
#
# Nothing below grants --privileged. Rootful is not privileged, and conflating the
# two is how a host quietly ends up with both.

# ── WITHOUT THIS FILE THE IMAGE IS NOT A FLEET HOST, AND SAYS NOTHING ─────────
#
# A bootc image runs systemd as PID 1 when it is BOOTED. Imported into WSL2 it
# does not: WSL starts its own `init` and systemd is simply never launched, so
# every quadlet in /etc/containers/systemd is inert and `systemctl
# is-system-running` answers `offline`. Measured 2026-09-05 on the first import of
# this image — `pid1=init(awnix)`, `/etc/wsl.conf` ABSENT — i.e. the "fleet host"
# could not have run a single unit, and nothing about the import said so: it
# booted, podman 5.8.5 was present, the quadlet directory was there, and it looked
# finished.
#
# Baked into the IMAGE rather than written after import on purpose. A post-import
# step is a per-machine ritual nobody writes down, which is the class this whole
# cutover exists to end; an image that carries it is correct on every import,
# including the ones nobody supervises.
#
# default=root because the fleet's 201 quadlets are system units under /etc, and
# an interactive session that lands as a non-root user cannot manage them.
#
# 🚨 REVERTED 2026-09-05, AND DO NOT RE-ADD IT WITHOUT READING THIS. Baking that
# file makes the imported WSL2 distro FAIL TO START — `wsl -d awnix` answers
# `Catastrophic failure / Wsl/Service/E_UNEXPECTED` and it stays `Stopped`.
#
# Measured by a controlled A/B, not inferred. The same image was built twice,
# differing ONLY in whether this layer is present, and both were imported and
# probed in one window:
#
#     awnix         wsl.conf systemd=true   -> Catastrophic failure
#     awnix-nosysd  no wsl.conf             -> RESPONDS, podman=yes
#     Debian        (the live fleet)        -> RESPONDS, 122 containers
#
# The control's image id was byte-identical to the pre-wsl.conf build, so the one
# line is the only variable.
#
# WHAT THIS MEANS, and it is the reason the line is a comment rather than a fix
# waiting to be finished: WITHOUT the file the distro starts but PID 1 is WSL's
# own `init`, so no quadlet runs. WITH it the distro does not start at all. Both
# ends are dead, so **WSL2 cannot host this fleet from a bootc image** and the
# rehearsal belongs on the ISO / bare-metal lane, which already exists, is gated,
# and is where a bootc image is designed to run.
#
# The image is left able to START, because a container image that produces a
# non-starting distro is worse than one that produces a limited one: the limited
# one can be inspected.
# RUN printf '[boot]\nsystemd=true\n\n[user]\ndefault=root\n' > /etc/wsl.conf

# The fleet drives podman through its systemd generator, so the generator and the
# socket both have to be present and enabled at BOOT, not started by hand — a host
# that needs a human to start its container engine is not self-managing, which is
# the property this whole cutover exists to gain.
RUN systemctl enable podman.socket || true

# The directory the quadlet generator READS. It is created empty and stays empty
# in the image: deploy-time generation fills it. Creating it here rather than
# letting the first deploy mkdir it means a failed deploy leaves an empty
# directory rather than an absent one, and "empty" is a state the gate below can
# actually judge.
RUN mkdir -p /etc/containers/systemd

# QDC001 (check_quadlet_dir_clean.py) gates that this directory holds units and
# NOTHING ELSE. On Debian it opens at 816 non-unit files and ratchets down; on a
# fresh awnix host it starts at zero and must stay there. An immutable host is
# what makes that pin permanent rather than aspirational: there is no durable
# place to drop a .bak-cafix copy, which is the file class that has already
# resurrected a retired genesis-lb service once.

LABEL com.aitheros.layer="aitheros-fleet" \
      com.aitheros.role="fleet-host" \
      com.aitheros.privilege-model="rootful" \
      com.aitheros.privilege-decision="option-a-reversible; see header. rootful != privileged" \
      com.aitheros.units="generated-at-deploy; none baked" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="AitherOS fleet host: awnix base plus a boot-enabled container engine. Carries no service units by design."
